@flexiarg workshop/distinguishing-cases-before-adoption
@title Distinguishing Cases Before Adoption
@keywords scope, counterexample, review, commissioning, cascade
@audience pattern authors, affected participants, reviewers, Layer 4 workshop authority
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade technique
@provenance Joe requested a Layer 4 process for managing pattern-language extensions, 2026-09-12; codex-25 operationalization of the discussion of Corneli (2016), An institutional approach to computational social creativity, https://metameso.org/~joe/papers/corneli2016institutional.pdf. Proposed local procedure, not a quotation from the paper.
@review Joe authorized adding this process layer; Joe alone currently adopts workshop-family institutional rules and sanctions. Affected participants advise and challenge; they do not thereby acquire adoption authority.
@class process
@domain process
@family-note workshop/ Layer 4 extension and maintenance process; see LAYER4-PROCESS.md for the linked sequence.
@status authored 2026-09-12; not assayed; no runtime enforcement claimed.

! conclusion:
  Before adopting a pattern change, preserve its motivating case and a
  nearby case where applying it would be wrong; review the distinction and
  the effect on patterns that depend on it.

  + context:
    A justified local refusal can become a universal prohibition. A green
    check can answer a narrower question than the claim made for it. A
    pattern added to a cascade can silently widen the cascade's scope.

  + IF:
    A candidate is proposed for adoption, or an existing pattern's scope,
    enforcement, or placement in a cascade is proposed to change.

  + HOWEVER:
    Some responses are exploratory and lack an executable detector.
    Examples support a bounded claim, not universal validity. Absence of a
    mechanical test must remain explicit rather than being filled by prose.

  + THEN:
    The owner supplies the originating observation, proposed response,
    expected outcome, and a nearby contrasting case where that response
    should not be required. A reviewer other than the author examines both,
    including whether they differ on the property the proposal claims.
    Record affected pattern versions and cascade edges, what each edge
    means, and any downstream scope that needs review. A missing contrast
    or unresolved dependency remains an explicit adoption gap.
    If mechanical enforcement is claimed, link the detector and actual gate,
    pin their versions, and retain both an induced-breach refusal and an
    admissible-case acceptance. A bookkeeping check warrants bookkeeping
    only. Record social review as social review. The adoption authority
    decides whether evidence supports the proposed scope; unsupported
    enforcement claims must be withheld or narrowed.

  + BECAUSE:
    Contrasting cases expose both failure to detect a breach and refusal
    where no breach exists. Reviewing cascade relationships prevents a
    locally supported change from acquiring unsupported downstream authority.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Use workshop/open-proposals-named-adoption for the recorded adoption decision.]
    next[Use workshop/revise-patterns-from-observed-use after a bounded application.]
    next[Keep detector existence, gate wiring, and commissioning as separate evidence obligations.]
